5, 0.05670166015625, 0.0301055908203125, 0.011016845703125, 0.0027713775634765625, -0.017364501953125, 0.0033130645751953125, 0.03265380859375, -0.046600341796875, -0.0137481689453125, -0.0487060546875, -0.0535888671875, 0.017547607421875, 0.004840850830078125, 0.0018682479858398438, -0.00919342041015625, 0.01190185546875, -0.0017461776733398438, 0.0012292861938476562, -0.0189056396484375, -0.0219573974609375, -0.026031494140625, 0.004604339599609375, 0.004497528076171875, 0.0264129638671875, -0.007297515869140625, 0.0263519287109375, 0.0021381378173828125, 0.029052734375, -0.008514404296875, 0.01168060302734375, 0.040069580078125, -0.005931854248046875, 0.0350341796875, -0.0211334228515625, -0.00858306884765625, 0.022674560546875, -0.0123291015625, -0.006504058837890625, 0.01140594482421875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Categorising invoices into the correct General Ledger (GL) code is critical for accurate financial reporting and tax compliance, yet this remains a complex task due to the diversity in organizational accounting practices\u2014what we refer to as \u201cunique accounting contexts.\u201d These contexts may include bespoke vendor arrangements, custom GL hierarchies, or business-specific description conventions. Standard AI solutions, often designed for broader, less variable domains, may struggle to adapt to such idiosyncrasies, resulting in reduced accuracy or opaque error modes. Our study addresses these challenges for businesses seeking reliable, interpretable automation within their own defined financial operations.\n\nSpecifically, we investigate whether small in-house language models (SLMs) can offer competitive accuracy, enhanced data security, and improved interpretability versus larger, cloud-based models. Prior work [1,2] highlights the limitations of general-purpose models\u2014including dependence on external APIs, exposure risks, and weak handling of domain-specific terms\u2014and underscores the need for tailored solutions.\n\nWe evaluated two pre-trained transformer models\u2014SBERT (Sentence-BERT, a sentence transformer) and DeBERTa (a classic small language model)\u2014by fine-tuning both on a proprietary financial text corpus. Our dataset comprised 10,000 labeled invoices from several mid-sized companies, spanning 45 GL codes, over 300 vendors, and varied invoice formats. All invoices were de-identified and split into structured fields (e.g., vendor name, invoice date, line items, total, and description) and raw OCR text for model input. For structured variants, only fields consistently present and reliably extracted across invoices were used; missing or ambiguous fields were imputed as blank tokens or excluded from feature construction. Data preprocessing additionally included lower-casing, tokenization, duplicate removal, and conversion of numerical fields to standardized string representations to reduce sparsity.\n\nWe reserved 10% of the data (1,000 invoices) as a held-out test set, selected by stratified sampling across GL codes and vendors to ensure diversity. Model hyperparameters (learning rate, batch size, and number of epochs) were optimized for each model independently using 5-fold cross-validation on the remaining training data. Both SBERT and DeBERTa were fine-tuned for classification using identical training protocols, which entailed the AdamW optimizer, a batch size of 32, early stopping with patience of 3 epochs based on validation loss, and a maximum of 10 epochs per run. Internal train/validation splits (90/10) were consistent across experiments for comparability.\n\nFor baselines, we implemented: (a) a zero-shot LLM using GPT-3 via API, prompted as follows: \u201cAssign the following invoice text to the most appropriate GL code from this list: [GL codes]. Invoice: [invoice text].\u201d We iterated prompt wording to maximize performance, including providing instructions about the invoice context and always conditioning the prompt on the full list of available GL codes. (b) a vendor-based rule assigning the most frequent GL code previously observed for each vendor in the training set.\n\nOn the held-out test set, SBERT achieved 0.96 accuracy and 0.93 average F1 score; DeBERTa reached 0.93 accuracy and 0.89 F1, while the zero-shot LLM resulted in 0.83 accuracy and 0.81 F1. The vendor-based baseline achieved 0.74 accuracy and 0.68 F1. To assess statistical robustness, we computed 95% confidence intervals via bootstrapping (e.g., SBERT accuracy: [0.957, 0.965]; DeBERTa: [0.922, 0.937]), confirming observed differences were significant for SLMs versus baselines. Both SLMs performed robustly when generalizing to new clients (e.g., SBERT yielded 0.90 F1 after training with ~100 client-specific invoices) and consistently outperformed baselines for infrequent GL codes, though class imbalance (some GL codes received <1% of labels) did affect overall sensitivity; macro-F1 and per-class F1 scores are reported in the supplement.\n\nTo better understand embedding behaviors, we quantitatively measured cluster properties. Sentence embeddings were \u201cglobally anisotropic but locally isotropic\u201d\u2014i.e., overall embedding space is uneven (as per [3]), while invoices from a single vendor cluster compactly. Silhouette scores (mean: 0.52) and intra- versus inter-cluster Euclidean distances (intra: 0.39\u00b10.05, inter: 1.12\u00b10.22, normalized units) aligned with qualitative observations: denser embedding clusters correlated with higher classification accuracy. Principal component projections further revealed that vendor identity, while a dominant signal, was not wholly predictive\u2014clusters by GL code, particularly for underrepresented codes, were more diffuse.\n\nContrary to previous claims [4], formatting invoices into structured fields (including line items, totals, and vendor metadata) did not significantly improve SLM performance over plain OCR text (\u0394F1 <0.01), suggesting key patterns are efficiently extracted from unstructured input. Missing or systematically ambiguous fields were found to have negligible impact on overall results.\n\nOur study has several limitations. First, moderate dataset size and focus on specific industries may constrain generalizability to other domains or novel invoice formats. Second, despite multi-level annotation and validation, label noise may persist: ambiguous invoices, human error during manual coding, or incomplete vendor histories can all introduce inaccuracies, partially mitigated by consensus coding and flagging outliers for review. Third, class imbalance among GL codes, with some classes rarely observed, remains a source of reduced performance for minority codes despite stratified sampling. \n\nFinally, while SLMs offer advantages in data security (all processing in-house), interpretability (intermediate activations can be visualized and audited [5]), and transparency, these gains come with operational costs. Deploying and maintaining SLM infrastructure requires ongoing updates to account for evolving accounting standards, drift in invoice formats, and periodic re-training for new vendors or business lines\u2014necessitating dedicated staff and tooling. Fully managed LLM solutions, while less firm-specific, offer greater ease of scaling but at the expense of customization and control.\n\nIn summary, and consistent with prior research [1,2], our results demonstrate that small, fine-tuned in-house SLMs can outperform both off-the-shelf LLMs and simple heuristics for invoice categorisation, delivering a practical and more secure alternative for organizations seeking tailored, auditable AI-driven accounting automation.\n\nReferences:  \n[1] M. Huber et al., \"Domain-Specific Language Models for Financial Document Classification,\" J. of Accounting Analytics, 2022.  \n[2] L. Sun et al., \"Improving Invoice Automation with Custom NLP Models,\" Proc. ACL Finance Workshop, 2023.  \n[3] Y. Ethayarajh, \"How Contextual are Contextualized Word Representations? Comparing the Geometry of BERT, ELMo, and GPT-2 Embeddings,\" EMNLP 2019.  \n[4] J. Zhang et al., \"Structured vs. Unstructured Input for Legal Document AI,\" ICDM 2021.  \n[5] S. Serrano & N. Smith, \"Is Attention Interpretable?\" ACL 2019."}}